# ==============================================================================
# Ecommerce Monorepo - Root BUILD.bazel
# ==============================================================================
# Workspace-wide package groups, license declaration and Gazelle wiring.
# ==============================================================================

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Gazelle configuration
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `bazel run //:gazelle` regenerates BUILD files and maps Go imports onto Bazel
# labels. These directives scope what it may touch; all of them apply to this
# directory and every subdirectory.
#
# `# gazelle:prefix` declares the import path that corresponds to this
# repository root. It must match the `module` line in go.mod. Without it Gazelle
# cannot tell that an import such as
# `github.com/ecommerce/services/commerce/internal/store` lives in this
# repository, so it shells out to `go`, which tries to reach github.com over the
# network and fails:
#     gazelle: finding module path for import github.com/ecommerce/... :
#     git ls-remote ... fatal: could not read Username for 'https://github.com'
# That made `bazel run //:gazelle` non-hermetic (it fails outright under
# --config=ci) and slow. Declaring the prefix keeps it offline.
#
# `# gazelle:map_kind` teaches Gazelle that this repository's macros expand to
# the rules it already knows. Gazelle does not expand macros: it matches rule
# *kinds* against a fixed list (`go_library`, `go_test`, `go_binary`,
# `go_proto_library`, ...). A target written as `go_lib(...)` was therefore
# invisible to Gazelle, which had two consequences, both observed here:
#
#   1. Gazelle could not index it, so it could not resolve an import of it and
#      fell through to the network lookup described above. `bazel run //:gazelle`
#      silently failed to add a `deps` entry for a *new* import, so the very
#      command meant to keep BUILD files in sync left them broken.
#   2. Gazelle generated a second, raw `go_library` beside each macro. Two targets
#      in one Bazel package may not share a Go import path, so that copy had to
#      carry no `deps` and then failed to compile with
#      "missing strict dependencies". The stray `*_lib` targets that used to sit
#      next to `go_service`, `devtool_bin` and `go_lib` were Gazelle's output, not
#      hand-written code - deleting them was not enough, `bazel run //:gazelle`
#      recreated them within minutes.
#
# The syntax is `# gazelle:map_kind <from_kind> <to_kind> <load_file>`, where
# <from_kind> is the kind Gazelle knows natively, <to_kind> is the name written
# into BUILD files, and <load_file> is the .bzl Gazelle attributes the generated
# `load()` to.
#
# Two things about map_kind are easy to get wrong, and both were wrong here.
#
# 1. <load_file> must be the .bzl that actually defines <to_kind>, which for
#    every macro in this repository is `//build:macros.bzl`. It was
#    `@rules_go//go:def.bzl`, so Gazelle emitted
#        load("@rules_go//go:def.bzl", "go_bin")
#    and rules_go exports no such symbol. Gazelle never checks that the symbol
#    exists, so a working BUILD file silently became an unloadable one on the
#    next `bazel run //:gazelle`.
#
# 2. Gazelle can only *index* one macro name per builtin kind, and the
#    constraints collide here. `config.KindMap` is keyed by FromKind, so
#    `map_kind go_library X` can appear only once, yet `metaResolver.Resolver`
#    selects an entry by comparing `mappedKind.KindName` against the rule's kind.
#    So a Go library is indexable under exactly one of `go_lib`, `logging_lib`,
#    `metrics_lib`, `tracing_lib`, `auth_lib`, `secrets_lib`, `policy_lib` - not
#    all seven. Whichever loses cannot be indexed, and then Gazelle cannot map
#    an import of it to a label.
#
# 3. An unindexable library is worse than a rewritten one. In a package Gazelle
#    manages, a missing index makes it silently *delete* the corresponding
#    `deps` entry rather than error, and then shell out to `go` to ask about the
#    import:
#        gazelle: finding module path for import
#        github.com/ecommerce/platform/observability/logging: go: -d flag is ...
#    That both breaks compilation (the dependency is gone) and makes the command
#    non-hermetic, so it fails outright under --config=ci.
#
# The resolution: the Go libraries in this repository all use `go_lib`, which is
# the one name Gazelle can both write and index. The platform capability macros
# (logging_lib, metrics_lib, ...) add nothing to the target except `tags` - and
# every check in //bazel/aspects:aspects.bzl keys on those tags, not on the rule
# kind - so //platform/* spells the same tags out explicitly on a `go_lib`. The
# resulting targets are identical: same rule, same importpath, same visibility,
# same tags.
#
# The capability macros remain defined in //build:macros.bzl for packages that
# are `# gazelle:ignore`d and therefore need no index. See their docstrings.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# gazelle:prefix github.com/ecommerce
# gazelle:proto disable
# gazelle:go_generate_proto false

# The three shapes Gazelle owns outright. `go_lib`, `go_bin` and `go_test_lib`
# are thin wrappers over `go_library`, `go_binary` and `go_test` that add only
# tags, an importpath and a visibility, all of which Gazelle already understands,
# so these one-way mappings are exact in both directions: Gazelle writes this
# name, and Gazelle can index a rule that already uses it.
#
# Note the direction on the test entry. Gazelle understands `go_test`, so it is
# `go_test_lib` that has to be taught what it is. Mapping `go_test_lib -> go_test`
# instead makes lookupMapKindReplacement walk in a circle and every Gazelle run
# aborts with
#     found loop of map_kind replacements: go_test -> go_test_lib -> go_test
# gazelle:map_kind go_library go_lib //build:macros.bzl
# gazelle:map_kind go_binary go_bin //build:macros.bzl
# gazelle:map_kind go_test go_test_lib //build:macros.bzl

# There is deliberately no map_kind for logging_lib, metrics_lib, tracing_lib,
# auth_lib, secrets_lib, policy_lib, devtool_bin, go_service, proto_lib or
# proto_go. See point 2: each would displace one of the three above from the
# index, and the losing name becomes unresolvable.

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Gazelle
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `bazel run //:gazelle` regenerates BUILD files and maps Go imports onto Bazel
# labels. `gazelle_binary.languages` takes go_library labels that export
# `NewLanguage`, not language-name strings.
load("@gazelle//:def.bzl", "gazelle", "gazelle_binary")

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# License compliance
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Use the canonical SPDX kinds shipped by rules_license rather than hand-rolling
# `license_kind` targets: a LICENSE file has exactly ONE license, so listing
# several kinds here (as this repo used to) mislabels the project.
load("@rules_license//rules:license.bzl", "license")

package(
    # Inherit the license declared below. Repeat
    # `default_applicable_licenses = ["//:license"]` in a package only when it
    # ships a *different* license than the workspace default.
    default_applicable_licenses = ["//:license"],
    default_visibility = ["//visibility:public"],
)

license(
    name = "license",
    package_name = "ecommerce",
    license_kinds = ["@rules_license//licenses/spdx:Apache-2.0"],
    license_text = "LICENSE",
    package_version = "0.1.0",
)

# Note: there used to be a second `license(name = "licenses")` here "retained for
# backwards compatibility". Two license targets describing one LICENSE file are
# two things that can drift, and nothing referenced the plural form any more.
# It has been removed rather than kept as a second source of truth.

gazelle_binary(
    name = "gazelle_bin",
    languages = [
        "@gazelle//language/proto:go_default_library",
        "@gazelle//language/go:go_default_library",
    ],
)

gazelle(
    name = "gazelle",
    gazelle = ":gazelle_bin",
)

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Workspace-wide package groups
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# There are none here, deliberately.
#
# This file used to declare seven of them - all_packages, platform_packages,
# libs_packages, proto_packages, services_packages, teams_packages and
# tools_packages - and nothing referenced a single one. They were also a second
# source of truth: //teams/BUILD.bazel already owns visibility for the whole
# repository, and `all_packages` was a verbatim duplicate of //teams:all. A
# visibility group that two files both define is a group that can drift.
#
# To add a visibility boundary, add it to //teams/BUILD.bazel. That is the only
# place a team, a layer or a shared surface is declared, which is what makes
# `bazel query 'rdeps(//..., //teams:commerce)'` a complete answer.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Repository metadata
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A single label for the files that define the build itself, so a packaging or
# release job can collect the workspace's configuration in one dependency
# instead of hard-coding a file list.
filegroup(
    name = "workspace_files",
    srcs = [
        ".bazelignore",
        ".bazelrc",
        ".bazelversion",
        ".gitignore",
        ".github/workflows/bazel.yml",
        "BUILD.bazel",
        "LICENSE",
        "MODULE.bazel",
        "MODULE.bazel.lock",
        "README.md",
        "docs/bazel-foundation.md",
        "go.mod",
        # The stamping script and its contract test. Both are listed because the
        # script runs on every build via --workspace_status_command, so a change
        # to it changes every stamped artifact and belongs in the same review as
        # a change to .bazelrc. These are cross-package labels, not paths:
        # //tools/dev is a Bazel package, so a root filegroup may not name its
        # files as //:tools/dev/...
        "//tools/dev:BUILD.bazel",
        "//tools/dev:workspace-status.sh",
        "//tools/dev:workspace-status_test.sh",
    ],
    visibility = ["//teams:platform"],
)

exports_files([
    "LICENSE",
    ".bazelrc",
    ".bazelversion",
    ".bazelignore",
    "go.mod",
])

# Note: there is deliberately no `//:default` target. An alias cannot expand to
# `//...`, and aliasing a `package_group` yields a target that cannot be built.
# Use `bazel build //...` and `bazel test //...` instead.
